  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 136 AM MDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nTuesday looks similar to Monday but with bulk shear values progged \nto increase to between 30 and 40kt over northern and western NM. A \nweak surface boundary will also be draped over northeast NM and this \nmodeled feature has been consistent for a few days now. There may be \nan elevated risk for a few strong to severe storms in this pattern, \nespecially across northern and northeast NM. The 00Z RRFS simulated \nreflectivity shows a lot more organized convection with 12-hr QPF \nfootprints in the 2 to 3\" range from the stronger cells over the \nnortheast plains. \n\nBy Wednesday, the next upper level low slides south down the West \nCoast into NorCal while the upper level ridge to our east attempts \nto squeeze westward again. This will tighten up the monsoon plume \nover the southwest CONUS, especially over NM. Abundant moisture is \nshown surging north Wednesday thru Friday with near-record daily \nPWATs for mid September. Several subtropical perturbations are also \nshown moving north from MX. A multi-day period of more widespread \nrainfall is anticipated. Some of this may come as bouts of showers \nand storms mixed with periods of rain and thick cloud cover. \n\nWPC QPF amounts are impressive over the next 7 days with widespread \nvalues between 1 and 2\" and locally higher than 3\" possible around \nthe high terrain. Even the 00Z LREF 7-day QPF shows everyone picking \nup at least 0.50\" by the end of the week (50th %tile). The risk for \nflooding will increase with each day of heavy rainfall, particularly \naround burn scars, urban areas, and low-lying flood prone sites. \nTemps will trend cooler and actually a little below normal for mid\nSeptember.",
